    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 121851\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1051 AM PST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Warm and mild weather will come to an end on Friday as cool,\n  unsettled weather returns to the region.\n\n* Additional precipitation arrives into the next work week with\n  only short breaks between systems.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nThursday looks to be the last day of our calm and mild weather  \ntrend. Highs will peak in the 70s and 80s across the lower \nelevations of the region with light to moderate winds and a slow \nbuild of higher cloud cover. The pattern changes on Friday with \nchances for snow and stronger winds pushing into the eastern \nSierra by the late morning. A Winter Storm Warning is in effect \nfor elevations above 7,000 feet in the Eastern Sierra for Friday \ninto Saturday afternoon. Around a foot of new snow will be \npossible in the Sierra Crest, and 5 to 10 inches of snow expected \nbetween 7,000 ft and 9,500 ft. Rain chances and increasing winds \nspread across the region into Friday afternoon. The strongest \nwinds arrive before precipitation, but breezy to gusty winds will \nlast through much of the day. A Wind Advisories are in effect \nFriday into early Saturday with 20 to 30 mph sustained southerly \nwinds and gusts to 50 mph. Winds across the region ease before \nrain fully exits the area, but calmer and cooler conditions are \nexpected for the rest of Saturday and through early Monday.\n\nMonday's precip arrives from the west as a low pressure builds\ninto the area early that morning and spreads across the region \nthrough the day. As of the current forecast, snow in the upper \nelevations of the Sierra from this system looks to stay relatively\nlight as the feed of moisture doesn't establish itself more \nconsistently until the system moves more to the east. Rain becomes\nwidespread across the rest of the CWA into the afternoon with \nlight to moderate rain rates. This low pressure looks to slow over\nthe region leading to rain chances lasting into early Wednesday. \nRain rates look to stay light but will add up over time. The break\nfrom rain looks to be short lived as another system will be \npossible late in the Wednesday and offer scattered showers into \nthe early weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds\nwill remain at or below 8 knots, following typical, daily \ndirectional trends through late Friday morning. Southeasterly \nwinds will pick up late tomorrow morning, gradually veering around\nto the southwest by late afternoon/early evening. Expect 10 to 15\nknot sustained winds with 20 to 25 knot wind gusts tomorrow \nafternoon into tomorrow evening. VFR conditions will prevail with \nincreasing high clouds beginning this evening.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds across most of \nthe region will remain around 5 to 10 knots and follow typical, \ndaily directional patterns. Exceptions include the northern Owens \nValley where breezy upvalley winds will pick up with gusts to \naround 25 to 30 knots this afternoon at KBIH, the western Mojave \nDesert where intermittent gusts to around 20 knots are possible \nthis afternoon at KDAG, and along the Colorado River Valley near \nKIFP where terrain- enhanced northerly winds will gust to around \n20KT late morning into early afternoon. Winds across the area will\ndecrease this evening, remaining around 5 to 10 knots through the\novernight period. VFR conditions otherwise continue, with \nincreasing high clouds beginning late this afternoon/early this \nevening.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Stessman\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
